== FIXED (Slice 76, v1.2.3) — already resolved by the merged symbol-escaping work ==
This was a behaviour of the pre-merge Release-1.2.0 line, whose history branched
(at e11aafb) BEFORE the readable symbol-name escaping slices.  Merging the
printer/reader slice line onto master brought in symbol_name_needs_escape
(printer.c), which escapes a name that READS AS A NUMBER in the current
*PRINT-BASE*: token_is_number_like covers base 10 (integers, ratios, floats) and
token_is_number_in_base covers bases /= 10.  An all-digit name is therefore
wrapped in |…| and reads back as a symbol.  Verified on v1.2.2:
  (prin1-to-string '|1234|)                               => "|1234|"
  (type-of (read-from-string (prin1-to-string '|1234|)))  => SYMBOL
  (prin1-to-string '|-5|)  => "|-5|"   (prin1-to-string '|3/4|) => "|3/4|"
The same path also fixed must-printer F229 (FACE under *PRINT-BASE* 16).  No code
change needed in this slice; closing as resolved by the merge.

----- original report -------------------------------------------------------
*print-escape* t
*print-base* 10.
*print-radix* nil
==> symbols containing only digits need to be escaped to be readable back as symbols!


bocl> *print-escape*

T
bocl> '|1234|

1234
bocl> (type-of (read-from-string (prin1-to-string '|1234|)))

(INTEGER -9223372036854775808 9223372036854775807) ; bad
bocl> (lisp-implementation-type)

"BOCL"


cl-user> *print-escape*
t
cl-user> '|1234|
|1234|
cl-user> (type-of (read-from-string (prin1-to-string '|1234|)))
symbol  ; good
cl-user> (lisp-implementation-type)
"Clozure Common Lisp"
